Helm scenario: HTTP 503 after a Helm upgrade
Your team deployed a new application version using Helm.
helm upgrade web-prod ./web-app -n prod
After the upgrade, users report HTTP 503 errors.

You check the Pods:
NAME                       READY   STATUS
web-app-7d9f8c6b5-x2abc     0/1     Running
web-app-7d9f8c6b5-y3def     0/1     Running

Both Pods are running, but neither is ready to receive traffic.

Step 1: Understand what this tells you
- Running means the container has started running; it does not guarantee that the application is healthy.
- 0/1 Ready means the Pod has one container, and that container is not ready.
- HTTP 503 means the request could not be served. One common cause is that the Service or Ingress has no healthy backend Pods, although the exact cause depends on where the 503 originates.
Step 2: Investigate in the right order
Run these commands:

------------------------------------------Checks-------------------------------------------------------------------


1. Check the Pod's readiness failure and events
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n prod

Look for messages such as Readiness probe failed, connection refused, HTTP status errors, or missing configuration.

2. Check application logs
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n prod
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n prod --previous

The second command helps if the container has restarted and previous logs are available.

3. Check whether the Service has ready backends
kubectl get svc -n prod
kubectl get endpointslices -n prod
kubectl get pods -n prod --show-labels

If the Service has no usable endpoints, check its selector against the Pod labels and investigate why the Pods are not ready.

4. Check the Helm release and deployed configuration
helm status web-prod -n prod
helm history web-prod -n prod
helm get values web-prod -n prod
helm get manifest web-prod -n prod


These help you identify what changed in the upgrade, including image versions, probe configuration, ports, selectors, and application settings.

5. Decide whether to fix forward or roll back

If the new release caused the outage and a known-good revision exists, consider:
helm rollback web-prod <previous-revision> -n prod --wait

Confirm the correct revision with helm history first. After recovery, fix the chart or values file and test the corrected release.
